  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1134 AM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nThe upper low that affected the region recently is over KS but\nstalling out and it will leave an elongated positively tilted\ntrough with a brief cutoff low southeast of the CWA. This will\nhelp to push through a back door cold front through tonight and\nbring some low clouds and gusty east to northeast winds. Winds on\nthe west slopes could gust over 30 mph and then tomorrow along the\nI-10 corridor we will see winds of 15-25G35mph. The low clouds \nwill make it to near the RGV and possibly make it near Deming to \nColumbus in the morning. Forecast soundings and CAMs indicate a \nmodestly deep moist layer so could see some showers on the east \nslopes of the Sacs and then some forcing with the upper low over \nfar west TX. Not expecting more than sprinkles into El Paso \ncounty, but eastern Hudspeth could see a tenth of an inch. The \neasterly winds will keep temperatures below normal with areas out \neast where clouds linger all day not getting warmer than the mid \n60s and mid 70s the warmest over the lower elevations of NM. \n\nNot much change as a Rex Block sets up for Sat. The moisture will\nnot be as deep and we will see less shower activity and some\ngradual breaks in the clouds. Most of the lowlands will get into\nthe 70s although it may still struggle out east. \n\nThe blocking feature does break down by late Sunday but then we\nwill see a couple of troughs break down ridge to the west and\nbring additional precip chances. Nothing significant or really\nimpactful but some light rain showers will be possible across the\narea, particularly Mon and Wed. Some instability Wed showing up in\nthe models which could lead to some stronger thunderstorms.\nTemperatures will be warming to near normal for early next week.",
